WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.860
Take a mouse with no working rods or cones.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:07.720
It still responds to light.

3
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:12.360
Its body clock still shifts with day and night, and its pupils still narrow in

4
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.300
bright light. A small set of retinal ganglion cells is doing this.

5
00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:19.540
They carry their own light pigment, called melanopsin.

6
00:00:20.120 --> 00:00:24.180
In mice, they are the retina's main route for setting the body clock, and they drive

7
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:28.560
the pupil reflex. They report how bright the world is, not what is in it.

8
00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:34.090
These cells are called intrinsically photosensitive retinal ganglion cells, or

9
00:00:34.090 --> 00:00:40.150
ipRGCs. An ipRGC makes its own light response with melanopsin.

10
00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:44.810
It also relays signals that rods and cones send through bipolar cells.

11
00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:46.530
So it has two jobs.

12
00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.770
Hold on to that. It explains everything that follows.

13
00:00:50.883 --> 00:00:52.223
Where do these signals go?

14
00:00:53.003 --> 00:00:57.443
ipRGC axons reach the suprachiasmatic nucleus, the brain's master clock.

15
00:00:58.063 --> 00:01:00.803
Light arriving there keeps the clock in step with day and night.

16
00:01:01.243 --> 00:01:02.823
That is called photoentrainment.

17
00:01:03.363 --> 00:01:08.403
Other ipRGC axons reach the olivary pretectal nucleus, which narrows the pupil.

18
00:01:08.983 --> 00:01:10.483
The two routes can be pulled apart.

19
00:01:11.043 --> 00:01:15.443
Remove one genetically marked set of these cells, and the pupil reflex fails badly.

20
00:01:15.443 --> 00:01:17.243
The clock still entrains.

21
00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:19.733
How do we know the cells matter?

22
00:01:20.353 --> 00:01:21.353
Compare two experiments.

23
00:01:21.873 --> 00:01:23.333
Take away only melanopsin.

24
00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:28.093
The mice still entrain, but their clock shifts less after a brief light pulse, and

25
00:01:28.093 --> 00:01:30.673
in steady bright light their pupils slowly widen again.

26
00:01:31.413 --> 00:01:34.993
Now take away the cells themselves, and leave rods and cones intact.

27
00:01:35.773 --> 00:01:40.813
The clock and pupil deficits are larger, much like mice that lack melanopsin, rods

28
00:01:41.013 --> 00:01:42.013
and cones together.

29
00:01:42.767 --> 00:01:47.387
So rods and cones send their clock and pupil signals through these cells, and

30
00:01:47.387 --> 00:01:49.827
melanopsin holds the pupil steady in bright light.

31
00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:52.527
Taking out the pigment removes one job.

32
00:01:53.127 --> 00:01:54.627
Taking out the cells removes both.

33
00:01:55.287 --> 00:01:59.287
That is why removing the cells and removing the pigment answer different questions.

34
00:02:00.067 --> 00:02:04.667
When you can, remove them in adult animals, because loss during development can

35
00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:05.667
rewire targets.

36
00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:11.450
In mice, these cells also affect mood and learning, apparently by two separate

37
00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:14.670
routes. A smaller story asks about seeing patterns.

38
00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:19.830
Mice lacking rod and cone signalling could tell a grating from plain grey, and mice

39
00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.290
lacking melanopsin showed contrast deficits.

40
00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.630
That is very coarse vision, not normal acuity.

41
00:02:25.630 --> 00:02:29.130
The main job of these cells is still the clock and the pupil.
